BT 14 TL 56 780 Td
/F2 15 Tf (Week 3. Constructive alignment: the idea in one page) Tj T*
T*
/F1 11 Tf (Constructive alignment is a principle for designing university courses, introduced by) Tj T*
/F1 11 Tf (John Biggs in the 1990s and developed in the book Teaching for Quality Learning at) Tj T*
/F1 11 Tf (University \(Biggs and Tang\).) Tj T*
T*
/F1 11 Tf (The name joins two ideas. "Constructive" comes from constructivist learning theory:) Tj T*
/F1 11 Tf (students do not receive knowledge ready-made, they construct meaning through their own) Tj T*
/F1 11 Tf (learning activity. "Alignment" refers to what the teacher does: the teaching and) Tj T*
/F1 11 Tf (learning activities and the assessment tasks are deliberately matched to the intended) Tj T*
/F1 11 Tf (learning outcomes.) Tj T*
T*
/F1 11 Tf (The key design question therefore changes. Instead of asking "What topics will I) Tj T*
/F1 11 Tf (cover?", the teacher asks "What should students be able to do after this course, and) Tj T*
/F1 11 Tf (what do they need to do during the course in order to learn it?") Tj T*
T*
/F1 11 Tf (In an aligned course all parts of the system support the same learning. Students find it) Tj T*
/F1 11 Tf (hard to escape without learning what was intended, because the outcomes, the activities) Tj T*
/F1 11 Tf (and the assessment all demand the same kind of thinking.) Tj T*
ET
BT /F1 9 Tf 290 40 Td (1) Tj ET
endstream
endobj
