/F2 15 Tf (Writing intended learning outcomes) Tj T*
T*
/F1 11 Tf (An intended learning outcome is written from the point of view of the student, not the) Tj T*
/F1 11 Tf (teacher. "To introduce students to sorting algorithms" describes what the teacher will) Tj T*
/F1 11 Tf (do. "Students will be able to compare sorting algorithms and justify the choice for a) Tj T*
/F1 11 Tf (given data set" describes what students will be able to do.) Tj T*
T*
/F1 11 Tf (A good outcome has three parts: a verb that shows the level of understanding, the) Tj T*
/F1 11 Tf (content the verb is applied to, and the context or standard.) Tj T*
T*
/F1 11 Tf (The verb matters most. Low-level verbs such as "list", "name" or "define" can be) Tj T*
/F1 11 Tf (satisfied by memorising. High-level verbs such as "explain", "analyse", "apply to a new) Tj T*
/F1 11 Tf (case", "evaluate" or "hypothesise" require understanding. Vague verbs such as) Tj T*
/F1 11 Tf ("understand", "know" or "be familiar with" should be avoided, because nobody can observe) Tj T*
/F1 11 Tf (them and nobody knows how to assess them.) Tj T*
T*
/F1 11 Tf (A course usually needs only five or six outcomes. Too many outcomes cannot all be taught) Tj T*
/F1 11 Tf (and assessed properly, and the important ones are lost among the trivial ones.) Tj T*
