BT 14 TL 56 780 Td
/F2 15 Tf (What goes wrong: misalignment and surface learning) Tj T*
T*
/F1 11 Tf (Consider a course whose outcome says "students will be able to critically evaluate) Tj T*
/F1 11 Tf (research designs". The teaching is a series of lectures, and the examination asks) Tj T*
/F1 11 Tf (students to list the features of each design.) Tj T*
T*
/F1 11 Tf (The three components ask for three different verbs: evaluate, listen, list. Students) Tj T*
/F1 11 Tf (quickly discover that only "list" is rewarded. They memorise lists, pass the examination) Tj T*
/F1 11 Tf (and remain unable to evaluate a research design. The declared outcome has not been) Tj T*
/F1 11 Tf (achieved, although the grades look fine.) Tj T*
T*
/F1 11 Tf (This is the typical result of misalignment: the assessment pulls students towards a) Tj T*
/F1 11 Tf (surface approach. They are not lazy or dishonest; they respond rationally to the signals) Tj T*
/F1 11 Tf (of the system.) Tj T*
T*
/F1 11 Tf (Misalignment also damages motivation and trust. Students who prepared for deep questions) Tj T*
/F1 11 Tf (and received a memory test feel that effort was wasted. Students who memorised and) Tj T*
/F1 11 Tf (received a problem to solve feel that the examination was unfair. In both cases the) Tj T*
/F1 11 Tf (complaint is justified, because the course promised one thing and assessed another.) Tj T*
ET
